{{also|re-collection}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*leǵ-}}
Via {{bor|en|fr|récollection}} or the verb {{m|en|recollect}}, both from {{der|en|la|recollectus}}, the past participle of {{m|la|recolligere||to collect again}}, itself from {{m|la|re-}} + {{m|la|colligere||to gather}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɛkəˈlɛkʃən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkʃən|s=4}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-recollection.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# The act of recollecting, or [[recall]]ing to the memory; the act of [[recall]]ing to memory
#: {{syn|en|reminiscence|remembrance}}
# The power of recalling ideas to the mind, or the period within which things can be recollected; remembrance
#: ''Alas that distant event isn't within my '''recollection''' anymore.''
# That which is recollected; something called to mind; a [[reminiscence]].
#: ''One of his earliest '''recollections'''." - {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}.''
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|Catholicism}} The act or practice of collecting or concentrating the mind; [[concentration]]; [[self-control]].
#: ''From such an education Charles contracted habits of gravity and '''recollection'''.''
#: ''In a world filled with media and electronic devices, '''recollection''' can be difficult.''
# {{lb|en|Catholicism}} A spiritual [[retreat]], especially one that is short.

